# Line-ending policy.
#
# Why this file exists: without it, a Windows clone with `core.autocrlf=true`
# checks every text file out as CRLF. Two of this repo's own CI gates read
# `key=value` configuration line by line, and the trailing CR becomes part of
# the last field — `check_file_size_ratchet.sh` reports `unknown conf key ''`
# and `check_command_surface.sh` reports `unknown disposition ''`. Both pass on
# the identical tree checked out with LF. The gates are not wrong and the confs
# are not wrong; the checkout was.
#
# It also stops `git status` reporting files as modified when nothing but the
# line endings differ, which is how the problem surfaces before anyone runs a
# gate.

# Default: let git decide what is text, and normalise it to LF in the
# repository. Working-tree endings follow the platform unless overridden below.
* text=auto

# Anything a shell, or a script parsing it line by line, has to read must be LF
# in the working tree too — not just in the repository. These are the files the
# gates above consume.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.conf          text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf

# Toolchain and manifest files read by cargo, npm and rustup. Cargo rewrites
# `Cargo.lock` with LF on every platform, so pinning it here keeps the working
# tree from disagreeing with what the tool just wrote.
Cargo.toml      text eol=lf
Cargo.lock      text eol=lf
*.toml          text eol=lf
.nvmrc          text eol=lf

# Workflow and config files are consumed by Linux runners.
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf

# Binary: never normalise, never diff as text.
*.png           binary
*.jpg           binary
*.ico           binary
*.icns          binary
*.woff          binary
*.woff2         binary
*.exe           binary
*.dll           binary
*.wasm          binary
